ly 1.2 PPL of degradation, which is meaningful but not catastrophic.\n\nNow I need to estimate the training result and TEAL eval for kl_sparsity35_s42, starting with the data I have for the 30% and 40% configurations.\n\nLooking at the 35% sparsity level, it should fall between the 30% and 40% results, so linear interpolation puts the TEAL PPL around 9.6. The training sparsity will likely be slightly under the target at around 0.33, similar to how 30% achieved 0.285, and the final loss should be roughly 0.18.\n\nFor kl_sparsity45_s42, interpolating between 40% and 50% gives me a TEAL PPL near 11.0, with training sparsity around 0.43.\n\nNow I'm moving on to examine kl_sparsity25_s42_eval_teal.\n\nActually, let me reconsider this. If the worst tier shows no clear kink point, the PPL values across the 6-point sweep should follow a roughly linear pattern from 25% through 50%, which would give an R² around 0.98 for a linear fit—piecewise fitting would only marginally improve that to 0.99.\n\nFor MMLU, the data tells a different story. Looking at the values across sparsity levels, I'm seeing inconsistent behavior—the scores don't move monotonically, with some dips and plateaus that make it hard to fit a clean narrative about regime changes. The 35% point seems to show a non-monotonic dip, and the overall pattern across 25% through 45% doesn't align cleanly with the PPL trajectory.\n\nNow examining BCE at the 30% operating point, which is supposed to be the sweet spot. The KL baseline sits at 8.98 PPL, and if BCE lands in that [9.0, 10.0] range as the worst tier suggests, I'm looking at maybe 9.5 as a reasonable estimate. That's only a 0.52 PPL advantage over KL—nowhere near as compelling as the 50% gap I'd expect.\n\nFor LongBench, I have some dense baseline numbers and KL results at 30%, but the worst tier predictions are troubling: TEAL at 30% should match or nearly match KL, KL at 50% degrades sharply, and BCE with compression might actually outperform KL on certain tasks, which directly contradicts the paper's main argument. The multi_news result is particularly odd and needs investigation.\n\nLooking at MT-Bench, the sparse models would struggle even more with multi-turn coherence. Dense gets around 7.5, KL30 drops to 5.5, and KL50 becomes nearly unusable at 3.5. TEAL at 30% sits at 5.0, so KL's advantage shrinks to just 0.5 points—barely meaningful in practical terms.\n\nFor RULER, I'm starting to examine the worst-case scenario there as well. Most of plan_001 is wrapped up, so I'm moving on to compile the comprehensive worst-tier YAML configuration. I need to calculate the 3-seed statistics for KL30 uniform MMLU performance—pulling the mean and standard deviation from the three seed runs to populate those metrics. Now I'm laying out the remaining experimental configurations—the sparsity sweep points that still need results, the BCE baseline at the recommended threshold, and the three major benchmark evaluations across LongBench, MT-Bench, and RULER. I'll also need to account for seed expansions and statistical validation before synthesizing everything into the final paper narrative. Let me construct this as a comprehensive YAML output.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7979,"cache_read_input_tokens":49947,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":7979},"output_tokens":13,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"af779414-f62d-4487-867b-50dcbebfd6c4","uuid":"c00e2dae-430a-4718-9854-9aa55cfb517d"}
